  "11": {
    "description": "A1. Weeks 3–4. Days of the week and basic time expressions (today, tomorrow, yesterday). Simple routines described in present tense. Vocabulary of about 200 words. Short paragraphs of two or three closely related sentences.",
    "example": "Today is Monday. On Monday, I go to school. I eat breakfast in the morning. I have lunch at school. I come home in the afternoon. I do my homework. I eat dinner with my family. I sleep at night. Tomorrow is Tuesday. On Tuesday, I also go to school. On Wednesday, I have music class. On Thursday, I have sport. On Friday, I see my friends. On Saturday, I do not go to school. I play in the park. On Sunday, I stay home with my family. We eat a big lunch together. I like Sunday a lot.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Based on the passage, which activity comes between school and dinner on a normal weekday?",
      "options": ["A) Sleeping", "B) Eating breakfast", "C) Doing homework", "D) Going to the park"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Yesterday was Friday. I worked all day. I was tired in the evening. I went to bed early. Today is Saturday. I do not work on Saturday. I sleep late. I eat a slow breakfast. I read the news. I go for a walk in the park. I see many people. I see children and dogs. I sit on a bench. I eat an apple. In the afternoon, I cook lunch. I call my mom. We talk for a long time. Tomorrow is Sunday. Tomorrow I will see my friends. We will eat dinner together. Today I rest.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "On which day did the speaker do paid work, based on the passage?",
      "options": ["A) Today", "B) Tomorrow", "C) Yesterday", "D) Saturday"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "12": {
    "description": "A1. Week 4. Telling time and discussing daily schedules. Numbers to 100. Basic ordinals (first, second, third). Vocabulary near 225 words. Simple question words: when, what time.",
    "example": "I wake up at seven o'clock. I have breakfast at seven thirty. I leave the house at eight. School starts at nine. We have a break at eleven. I eat lunch at twelve. School ends at three. I come home at four. I do my homework from four to six. I eat dinner at seven. I read a book at nine. I go to bed at ten. What time is your dinner? My dinner is at seven thirty. What time do you sleep? I sleep at ten. The day is long. The day is full. I am tired at night.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "How many hours pass between when the speaker wakes up and when school starts?",
      "options": ["A) One hour", "B) Two hours", "C) Three hours", "D) Five hours"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    "example2": "There are four trains today. The first train leaves at six o'clock. The second train leaves at nine. The third train leaves at noon. The fourth train leaves at three. I want the second train. It leaves at nine. The trip is two hours. I will arrive at eleven. My friend will meet me at the station. We will eat lunch at twelve. We will walk in the park at one. We will see a film at three. The film ends at five. I will take the last train home. The last train leaves at six in the evening. I will get home at eight.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "How many hours after the first train does the speaker's chosen train leave?",
      "options": ["A) Two hours", "B) Three hours", "C) Six hours", "D) Nine hours"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "13": {
    "description": "A1. End of first month. Simple past tense introduced with regular verbs ('walked,' 'talked'). Describing what happened yesterday. Vocabulary approaching 275 words. Two or three simple sentences about a past event.",
    "example": "Yesterday I walked to school. The day was sunny. I walked with my friend. We talked about the weekend. We laughed a lot. At school, we worked on a project. We painted a picture. After school, we walked to the park. We played for two hours. We climbed a big tree. We watched the birds. Then we walked home. I helped my mom with dinner. We cooked rice and vegetables. I called my grandma after dinner. We talked for a long time. I finished my homework. I listened to music. I was happy. It was a very good day.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What can we infer about how the speaker spent their day yesterday?",
      "options": ["A) They stayed home alone all day", "B) They spent time with at least one other person", "C) They went to school by bus", "D) They played a sport by themselves"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    "example2": "Yesterday it rained all morning. I stayed inside. I watched the rain from the window. The garden looked very green. The flowers opened slowly. The leaves shined with water. I watched a small bird. The bird sat on a branch. The bird looked wet and cold. I made hot tea. I held the cup in my hands. My cat watched the rain too. My cat sat on the warm chair. In the afternoon, the sun came out. I walked in the wet garden. The grass was soft. The air smelled fresh. I picked one flower. I put the flower on the table. The day was quiet and good.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "Based on the passage, what most likely caused the garden to look very green?",
      "options": ["A) The flowers opening slowly", "B) The bird sitting on a branch", "C) The morning rain", "D) The speaker watching from the window"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "14": {
    "description": "A1. Month 1, weeks 3–4. Irregular past tense verbs (went, saw, had, ate). Short narratives about familiar events. Vocabulary near 325 words. Simple conjunctions 'and' and 'but' connect two clauses.",
    "example": "We went to the park yesterday. The day was warm and sunny. I saw a big dog. The dog was friendly, and I was happy. I gave the dog some bread, but the dog wanted my apple. I ate the apple myself. We sat on the grass and we watched the people. My sister had a kite. She ran with the kite, but the wind was small. The kite did not fly. We laughed. Then we ate ice cream. The ice cream was sweet, but it was very cold. My teeth hurt for a moment. We went home in the evening. We were tired but happy.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What does the passage suggest about the speaker's experience with the ice cream?",
      "options": ["A) They enjoyed it with no problems at all", "B) They did not eat it in the end", "C) Something about it was not perfect, even though they ate it", "D) They were happy because it was so cold"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "The team had a game yesterday. The game started at three. Many people came to watch. The sun was hot, but the team was ready. They ran fast. They played well. They worked together as one team. In the first half, they scored one goal. The other team scored one goal too. In the second half, the game was very close. Both teams ran and tried hard. At the end, the other team scored one more goal. Our team lost, but they were not sad. The coach said they had played their best. We went home together. We ate dinner and we talked about the game.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What does the passage suggest about how the team played, even though they lost?",
      "options": ["A) They played badly, and that is why they lost", "B) They played well, but the result was still a loss", "C) The other team did not score any goals at all", "D) The game ended with no winner declared"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "15": {
    "description": "A1. End of first month. Simple connected sentences about daily life, with 'and,' 'but,' and 'because.' Past and present tenses used. Vocabulary of about 375 words. Picture-book level — simple, concrete, and personal. Equivalent to children's earliest readers.",
    "example": "On Saturday, I went to the store with my mom. The store was big and busy. We took a basket and we walked slowly. We bought apples because I love apples. We bought bread because my dad eats bread every morning. We bought milk and eggs and rice. I like apples because they are sweet and crunchy. My mom likes bread because it is warm and soft. At the store, I saw my friend Lisa. She was with her dad. We said hello and we talked for a minute. Then my mom and I paid for the food. We carried the bags home. We were tired, but we were happy. I helped my mom in the kitchen.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Why does the speaker like apples, according to the passage?",
      "options": ["A) Because their mom bought them at the store", "B) Because they went to the store on Saturday", "C) Because apples are sweet and crunchy", "D) Because their dad eats bread every morning"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Long ago, there was a small village in the hills. The hills were green and the air was clean. The people in the village were poor, but they were not sad. They grew their own food. They had small fields of wheat and corn. They kept goats and chickens. They worked hard every day, but they also rested in the evening. They sat outside and they talked. The children played in the streets. When one family had a problem, the other families came to help. Nobody was alone in the village. The people were happy because they helped each other every day. The village was small, but it was full of life.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "According to the passage, what kept the villagers from being sad?",
      "options": ["A) They had a lot of money saved up", "B) They lived high up in the green hills", "C) They supported one another every day", "D) They had many goats and chickens"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "16": {
    "description": "A1. Month 2. Introducing simple future with 'will' and 'going to.' Making plans and predictions. Vocabulary near 425 words. Short, concrete paragraphs about familiar future events.",
    "example": "Tomorrow will be a busy day. I will wake up early at six o'clock. I will eat a big breakfast. After breakfast, I will go to school. School will start at eight. I will have four classes in the morning. At lunch, I am going to eat with my friends. We are going to sit outside if the weather is nice. After school, I am going to play football with my friends. We will play for two hours. I will come home at five. I will do my homework. We will eat dinner at six. After dinner, I am going to read my book. I will go to bed at ten. It will be a good day.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Based on the passage, which event happens just before the speaker plays football?",
      "options": ["A) Eating breakfast at home", "B) Going to bed at ten", "C) Going to school", "D) Reading a book before bed"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Next week, the team is going to travel to another city. They will leave on Monday morning. The trip will take six hours by bus. They will stay in a small hotel near the stadium. They will play three games there. The first game will be on Tuesday. The second game will be on Wednesday. The third game will be on Friday. If they win two games, they will come home as champions. If they win only one game, they will be happy too. The coach thinks they are ready. He says they have trained well all year. The players are excited and a little nervous. I will watch every game on TV.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What is the smallest number of wins the team needs to be champions, based on the passage?",
      "options": ["A) One game", "B) Two games", "C) Three games", "D) All of them"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "17": {
    "description": "A1. Month 2. Describing people's appearance and personality with basic adjectives (tall, short, kind, funny). Simple comparatives (bigger, faster). Vocabulary around 475 words. Very short personal descriptions.",
    "example": "My best friend is Maya. She is tall and she is funny. She has long brown hair and big eyes. She is kind to everyone at school. Her dog is bigger than my dog. Her dog is also older. I am shorter than Maya. I am quiet, but Maya is loud. She talks a lot, and I listen. We are different, but we are good friends. I am faster than Maya when we run. I am also better at math. Maya is better at music. She sings well. My family is also different. My brother is taller than my dad. My sister is younger than me. My mom is the kindest person I know.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "In what way does the speaker do better than Maya, according to the passage?",
      "options": ["A) The speaker is taller than Maya", "B) The speaker is funnier than Maya", "C) The speaker's dog is bigger than Maya's", "D) The speaker is faster and better at math"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    "example2": "Our town has many old and new things. The river is wider than the road. The road is older than the bridge. The bridge is the newest thing in our town. People built the bridge five years ago. The bridge is also the longest in the area. The river is the oldest. People say the river is the most beautiful place to walk. The trees by the river are very tall. They are taller than the houses. The houses near the river are smaller than the houses on the hill. The hill is the highest place in town. From the hill, you can see everything. The view is wonderful in the morning.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "Based on the passage, which of the following is the youngest in age?",
      "options": ["A) The river that runs through town", "B) The road through the town", "C) The bridge over the river", "D) The houses on the hill"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "18": {
    "description": "A1. Month 2. Simple directions and location vocabulary (turn left, go straight, next to the library). Asking and giving basic directions. Vocabulary near 525 words. Commands and imperatives appear.",
    "example": "Excuse me, where is the school? Go straight on this street. Walk for two blocks. Then turn left at the bank. The school is next to the park. You will see a big yellow building. The school is the yellow building. Where is the library? Go straight and turn right at the store. The library is on Main Street. It is after the small coffee shop and before the post office. Where is the hospital? Go to the bus stop. Take the number five bus. Get off at the third stop. The hospital is across from the bus stop. It is a tall white building. Thank you very much. You are welcome. Have a good day.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "If you walk toward the library on Main Street from the speaker's directions, what do you pass just before reaching it?",
      "options": ["A) The school", "B) The park", "C) The small coffee shop", "D) The post office"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "To find the museum, follow these steps. First, walk down this street to the river. The river is at the end of the street. Then turn right at the river. Walk along the river for ten minutes. You will see an old church on your left. Keep walking. After the church, you will see a small bank. The museum is between the old church and the bank. The museum is a gray stone building. It has a red door. Do not cross the bridge. The bridge is on the other side of the river. If you cross the bridge, you will go too far. The museum opens at nine in the morning.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "Which two buildings stand on either side of the museum, based on the directions?",
      "options": ["A) The bank and the bridge", "B) The church and the bank", "C) The river and the path", "D) The church and the bridge"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "19": {
    "description": "A1. Month 2–3. Shopping and money vocabulary. Simple transactional dialogues. Prices, quantities, and requests. Vocabulary around 575 words. Very short functional exchanges.",
    "example": "Hello. Welcome to the store. How can I help you? I would like some apples, please. How much do they cost? Apples cost five dollars for one kilo. I would like two kilos. That will be ten dollars. I would also like some bread. The bread costs three dollars. Can I have one loaf? Yes, of course. Anything else? I would like a small bottle of milk. The milk is two dollars. So that is fifteen dollars in total. Here is twenty dollars. Here is your change. Five dollars back. Thank you very much. Have a good day. Goodbye. The shopping was easy. The food was fresh. I went home happy with my full bag.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What is the total cost of all the items the speaker buys at the store?",
      "options": ["A) Ten dollars", "B) Twelve dollars", "C) Fifteen dollars", "D) Twenty dollars"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "I went to a small shop today. I wanted to buy some books and pens. The first book I saw cost ten dollars. It was a story book. The second book cost seven dollars. It was about animals. I chose the story book because I love stories. I also wanted a pen. The blue pen cost three dollars. The red pen cost two dollars. I chose the blue pen. I had fifteen dollars in my pocket. I gave the money to the woman. She gave me my change. I got two dollars back. I put the book and the pen in my bag. I felt happy. I would still have some money left for tomorrow.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "How much money will the speaker have left after buying the chosen book and pen?",
      "options": ["A) One dollar", "B) Two dollars", "C) Three dollars", "D) Five dollars"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "20": {
    "description": "A1. Month 3. Weather and seasons vocabulary. Describing current and seasonal weather. Modal 'can' introduced for ability. Vocabulary near 625 words. Short descriptive passages about the natural world.",
    "example": "Today it is sunny and warm. The sky is blue. The wind is soft. I can wear a light shirt. In the summer, the days are long and hot. I can swim in the lake. I can play outside until late. In the autumn, the leaves turn yellow and red. The wind is cool. I can wear a light jacket. In the winter, it is cold and it often snows. The ground is white. I can ski with my friends. I can also drink hot tea by the fire. In the spring, the air is fresh. The flowers come back. The birds return. I like spring the best because everything is alive and beautiful again.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Which season does the speaker most clearly express a positive feeling about, and why?",
      "options": ["A) Summer, because the days are long and hot", "B) Winter, because they can ski with friends", "C) Spring, because everything comes back to life", "D) Autumn, because the leaves change color"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Bears live in the forest. They are big and strong. In the autumn, bears eat a lot. They eat berries, nuts, fish, and honey. They eat all day long. They get fat and ready for winter. When the snow comes, bears find a safe place. They sleep all winter long in a warm cave or a hole. They do not eat for many months. They live on their fat from autumn. In the spring, when the snow melts, bears wake up. They are very hungry. They go down to the rivers. They can catch fish with their big paws. They can also climb trees. Baby bears stay close to their mother for the whole first year.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "Why do bears probably eat a lot of food in autumn, based on the passage?",
      "options": ["A) Because rivers have more fish in autumn than in other months", "B) Because they need food stored as fat for the long winter sleep", "C) Because spring food is hard for them to find in the wild", "D) Because their paws are stronger after eating in autumn"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
